{"schema": "codereview.benchresult/1", "config": "panel-baseline-2026-11-04", "config_sha256": "3a2395618f00d63a81c194bdb2739483caafdbf1b85b4178adb1ed95348a2ec4", "case_id": "eval-user-input", "split": "public", "category": "injection", "created_at": "2026-11-05T00:39:17+00:00", "gate": "blocked", "degraded": false, "duration_ms": 21295, "calls": 3, "cost_usd": 0.0, "patch_sha256": "aede29b4f06cc7222354725727c4e4b67f6be642c2054ede221eb92a6a0687d9", "findings": [{"id": "604ef49d36f6", "rule": "", "severity": "blocker", "category": "security", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 7, "title": "Direct eval of request expression enables arbitrary code execution", "evidence": "The endpoint now returns 'eval(expr)' after only checking length, so any caller who can supply the expression parameter can execute arbitrary Python code within the server process instead of being limited to calculator syntax.", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Do not use Python eval on user-controlled input. Restore the dedicated parser, or replace it with a safe expression evaluator that only permits numeric literals and approved operators/functions via an AST whitelist.", "confidence": 0.99, "sources": ["openai"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded", "dispute": {"by": "nvidia", "family": "nvidia", "raiser_family": "openai", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "reason": "The code region shows the function replaced a safe parser with eval, but the length check (len(expr) > 200) remains active and blocks input exceeding 200 characters, preventing arbitrary code execution via the eval call.", "adequacy": "weak"}, "adjudication": {"role": "tech_lead", "seat": "deepseek", "family": "deepseek", "tier": "paid", "call_timeout": 900, "probe_rounds": 0, "decision": "sustain_finding", "rationale": "The function now hands caller-supplied `expr` straight to Python's `eval`, so any length-compliant payload (e.g. `__import__('os').system('id')`, well under 200 characters) executes arbitrary code in the server process on supported POSIX/Python 3.11+ deployments. The refutation's claim that the 200-character length check prevents code execution is unsound, and its quote merely restates the line the finding already cites, so it carries no counter-evidence; the defect stands at blocker severity.", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "positions": {"raiser": {"family": "openai", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "claim": "Direct eval of request expression enables arbitrary code execution"}, "refuter": {"seat": "nvidia", "family": "nvidia", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "reason": "The code region shows the function replaced a safe parser with eval, but the length check (len(expr) > 200) remains active and blocks input exceeding 200 characters, preventing arbitrary code execution via the eval call.", "adequacy": "weak"}}, "severity_applied": "blocker"}, "cross_family": "tech_lead_sustained"}, {"id": "f4f5e13e733a", "rule": "", "severity": "blocker", "category": "security", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 7, "title": "eval() on user-supplied expression is arbitrary code execution", "evidence": "The only guard is a length check ('if len(expr) > 200:', line 5) and then the raw string is evaluated: 'return eval(expr)'. Any caller of the calculator endpoint can pass e.g. __import__('os').system('id') or open('/etc/passwd').read(), all well under 200 characters. eval runs in this module's global scope, so even 'parse_expression' and other imported names are reachable. This converts a pure expression evaluator into a general-purpose interpreter over attacker-controlled input.", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Do not use eval. Restore parse_expression(expr) (or an equivalent AST-walking evaluator that only permits numeric literals and a whitelisted operator set). If a generic evaluator is truly required, pass explicit empty __builtins__ and a validated AST, but restoring the parser is the correct fix here.", "confidence": 0.98, "sources": ["deepseek"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded", "cross_family": "confirmed"}, {"id": "a0a30177d813", "rule": "eval-exec", "severity": "blocker", "category": "security", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 6, "title": "eval/exec call in added code", "evidence": "return eval(expr)", "evidence_quote": "return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Replace dynamic evaluation with a safe, explicit code path.", "confidence": 0.9, "sources": ["deterministic"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded"}, {"id": "88131b286537", "rule": "", "severity": "major", "category": "security", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 7, "title": "eval() used instead of safe parser, enabling code execution", "evidence": "return eval(expr)", "evidence_quote": "return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Restore parse_expression or implement strict input validation and a safe evaluation sandbox; never use eval on untrusted user input.", "confidence": 0.95, "sources": ["nvidia"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded", "cross_family": "confirmed"}, {"id": "65ca42357cd3", "rule": "", "severity": "minor", "category": "reliability", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 7, "title": "200-char length cap no longer bounds evaluation cost", "evidence": "'return eval(expr)' in combination with the existing 'if len(expr) > 200:' guard: the guard bounds input length, not work. A short input such as 9**9**9**9 or 10**10**10 passes the check and makes CPython build an astronomically large integer, consuming CPU and memory until the worker is killed. A hand-written parser with an operator/cost policy would reject or bound such input, so this is a resource-exhaustion regression introduced by the switch to eval.", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Once eval is removed, ensure the parser or evaluator enforces limits on exponentiation magnitude / operation count, or reject ** with large operands.", "confidence": 0.6, "sources": ["deepseek"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded"}, {"id": "0451af894f22", "rule": "", "severity": "minor", "category": "correctness", "file": "app/calc.py", "line": 7, "title": "Error and return-type contract changed for all callers", "evidence": "eval code: 'return eval(expr)'. Previously invalid input surfaced as whatever parse_expression raised (presumably ValueError, consistent with the explicit 'raise ValueError(\"expression too long\")' above it); now the same input raises SyntaxError/NameError/TypeError/ZeroDivisionError, and any caller catching ValueError will no longer handle bad input, surfacing as an unhandled 500. The return type also widens beyond a number: eval('True') returns a bool and eval(\"'abc'\") returns a str, so the endpoint can now return non-numeric results it never could before.", "evidence_quote": "    return eval(expr)", "recommendation": "Revert to parse_expression so the documented error type and numeric return type are preserved; if the parser must be replaced, wrap evaluation and re-raise as ValueError, and validate that the result is a number before returning it.", "confidence": 0.75, "sources": ["deepseek"], "consensus": false, "grounding": "grounded"}], "reviewer_reports": [{"provider": "nvidia", "lens": "operations", "status": "ok", "attempts": 1}, {"provider": "openai", "lens": "correctness", "status": "ok", "attempts": 1}, {"provider": "deepseek", "lens": "correctness", "status": "ok", "attempts": 1}]}